{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And she saw him, that he was good.</b> That the entire house was filled with light, as it is stated (Genesis 1:4) ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָאוֹר כִּי טוֹב״. And for this reason she hid him, for she feared lest the Egyptians see that light, and through the light they would also see the child, and therefore she hid him more than all the other infants. And further, lest they perceive the extraordinary nature of the light, for he is the redeemer who brings them out from darkness to light and from servitude to redemption. And perhaps she thought that for this reason the light was created with him, so that she might learn from it to hide him — like the primordial light that was stored away for the righteous in the time to come." "<b>And she hid him three months.</b> A hint that the light of the Torah was concealed until the third month, which is Sivan, as they expounded from the verse ״יִצְפֹּן לַיְשָׁרִים תּוּשִׁיָּה״ (Proverbs 2:7); therefore ״לֹא יָכְלָה עוֹד הַצְּפִינוֹ״. And see further below (Exodus 3:5) on the verse ״שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ מֵעַל רַגְלֶיךָ״, that Moses was stripped of materiality entirely, and he radiated from both his portions, and his human abode was filled with light, and this sign will testify to his essence in the future. And it appears that concerning the existence of this light that was born with him it is written ״וַתִּפְתַּח וַתִּרְאֵהוּ אֶת הַיֶּלֶד״ — what is ״וַתִּרְאֵהוּ״? Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 12b) that she saw the Shekhinah with him — and the Shekhinah, who mentions its name? Rather, ״וַתִּרְאֵהוּ״ refers to the presence of the light that was mentioned earlier in his saying ״כִּי טוֹב הוּא״, and that light she saw with the child, for אֶת serves in place of עִם, and through this he appeared like a grown youth, for the child and the light were joined together; therefore it says ״וַתִּרְאֵהוּ״ — that is to say, the light — אֶת הַיֶּלֶד — with the child."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "The Aramaic phrase מָאן דְּכַר שְׁמַהּ is rendered literally as 'who mentions its name' (i.e., who explicitly names it); the rhetorical question challenges whether 'Shekhinah' is itself the object she saw."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:54.834445764Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}